  "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x-y)}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)\\cancel{(x-y)}}{x\\cancel{(x-y)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x} & =\\frac{-1+(-1)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
